<puzzle-view class="w-full">
  <div class="flex gap-12">
    <article class="mx-auto w-full min-w-0 max-w-5xl">
      <header class="mb-8">
        <h1 class="text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">Markdown</h1>
        <p class="mt-2 text-body">
          Renders a markdown string as semantic HTML — a marked-lexer token walker building DOM
          with createElement/createTextNode, so markdown can never inject markup; CommonMark plus
          GFM tables, task lists, and strikethrough.
        </p>
      </header>

      <ExampleBox code={ codeHero }>
        <div class="w-full max-w-2xl self-start">
          <Markdown value={ sampleMd }/>
        </div>
      </ExampleBox>

      <section id="installation" class="mt-12 scroll-mt-20">
        <h2 class="mb-4 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">Installation</h2>
        <p class="mb-3 text-sm text-body">
          Copy the piece into your app — it pulls in lib/markdown-doc.js and needs marked for
          tokenizing:
        </p>
        <CodeBlock code={ installCmd }/>
        <CodeBlock code={ installNpm } class="mt-3"/>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-muted">
          The <code class="rounded bg-surface-sunken px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[13px] text-ink">^17</code> pin is deliberate:
          @tiptap/markdown — the dependency behind the Markdown Editor piece — pins marked ^17,
          so installing ^18 here would give an app that uses both pieces two copies of the
          parser in its bundle.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="usage" class="mt-12 scroll-mt-20">
        <h2 class="mb-4 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">Usage</h2>
        <CodeBlock code={ usageImport }/>
        <CodeBlock code={ usageMarkup } class="mt-3"/>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-body">
          The <code class="rounded bg-surface-sunken px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[13px] text-ink">value</code> prop is a plain markdown string the parent owns; an
          empty or whitespace-only string renders nothing.
          Pass <code class="rounded bg-surface-sunken px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[13px] text-ink">newTab</code> to open scheme-bearing (external) links in a new
          tab with rel=noopener noreferrer — relative links are left alone.
          Any <code class="rounded bg-surface-sunken px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[13px] text-ink">class</code> is appended to the root, so width and prose
          constraints are yours to set.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock code={ usageNewTab } class="mt-3"/>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-muted">
          Content renders client-side only. The walker builds DOM inside
          an <code class="rounded bg-surface-sunken px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[13px] text-ink">island</code> after mount,
          so on prerendered (static or hybrid) pages the markdown appears after
          takeover rather than in the baked markup — worth weighing for content you need indexed.
        </p>
      </section>

      <div class="mt-12 space-y-12">
        <section id="supported-syntax" class="scroll-mt-20">
          <h2 class="mb-4 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">Supported syntax</h2>
          <p class="mb-3 text-sm text-body">
            CommonMark — headings, paragraphs, emphasis, inline code, links, images, blockquotes,
            fenced code with a language, ordered and unordered lists with nesting, and horizontal
            rules — plus the GFM additions marked's lexer handles: tables with per-column
            alignment, task lists, and strikethrough. Character entities in prose are decoded
            through a small named table (the walker writes text nodes, so the browser never gets
            a chance to decode them for us).
          </p>
          <p class="mb-3 text-sm text-body">
            Raw HTML inside the markdown renders as literal visible text, by design. There is no
            escape hatch that turns a string into markup — that is the whole point of the piece.
          </p>
          <p class="mb-3 text-sm text-body">The example above renders this markdown:</p>
          <CodeBlock code={ sampleMd }/>
        </section>

        <section id="security" class="scroll-mt-20">
          <h2 class="mb-4 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">Security</h2>
          <p class="mb-3 text-sm text-body">
            Untrusted markdown — comments, product descriptions, anything a user typed — is the
            expected input. Every node is built with document.createElement and
            document.createTextNode; the piece never assigns innerHTML, so no amount of markdown
            can produce an element the walker did not decide to create.
          </p>
          <ul class="mb-3 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5 text-sm text-body">
            <li>
              Link hrefs run through safeUrl: javascript:, data:, and vbscript: URLs (including
              tab/newline-obfuscated spellings) collapse to #.
            </li>
            <li>
              Image srcs run through safeImageUrl, which is stricter — relative, http, https, and
              data:image/ only; a rejected or empty source renders no image at all.
            </li>
            <li>
              Code-fence language tags become a language-* class, so they are filtered to a plain
              identifier before they reach a class attribute.
            </li>
            <li>
              Entities are decoded by a fixed named table plus validated numeric code points —
              never by round-tripping through innerHTML.
            </li>
            <li>Raw HTML tokens are emitted as text, so an injected tag shows up as text.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="text-sm text-muted">
            Titles and alt text are inert attribute values; ordered-list start and task-item
            checked state are numeric and boolean.
          </p>
        </section>
      </div>
    </article>

    <Toc class="sticky top-24 hidden h-fit w-48 shrink-0 self-start xl:block" items={ toc }/>
  </div>
</puzzle-view>

<script>
import { PuzzleView } from '@magic-spells/puzzle';
import Markdown from '@/components/ui/Markdown.pzl';
import ExampleBox from '@/components/docs/ExampleBox.pzl';
import CodeBlock from '@/components/docs/CodeBlock.pzl';
import Toc from '@/components/docs/Toc.pzl';

// Docs page for Markdown — follows the ButtonDoc exemplar shape. Every markdown
// and code sample lives here as a template-literal const: the compiler parses
// template prose, so raw `#`, backticks, and angle brackets must never sit
// inline in the markup above.
const installCmd = 'puzzle add piece markdown';
const installNpm = 'npm install marked@^17';
const usageImport = `import Markdown from '@/components/ui/Markdown.pzl';`;
const usageMarkup = `<Markdown value={ article.body } />`;
const usageNewTab = `<Markdown value={ comment.body } newTab={ true } class="max-w-prose" />`;
const codeHero = `<Markdown value={ sampleMd } />`;

// Small inline SVG so the image row needs no network asset — data:image/ is one
// of the schemes safeImageUrl allows through.
const BADGE =
	'data:image/svg+xml;base64,PHN2ZyB4bWxucz0naHR0cDovL3d3dy53My5vcmcvMjAwMC9zdmcnIHdpZHRoPSc5NicgaGVpZ2h0PSczMic+PHJlY3Qgd2lkdGg9Jzk2JyBoZWlnaHQ9JzMyJyByeD0nNicgZmlsbD0nc2xhdGVibHVlJy8+PGNpcmNsZSBjeD0nMTYnIGN5PScxNicgcj0nNycgZmlsbD0nd2hpdGUnLz48L3N2Zz4=';

const SAMPLE_MD = `# Release notes

Ships **today** with *smaller* output and ~~three~~ zero runtime deps.
Read the [full changelog](https://example.com/changelog), or run \`npm outdated\`.
Entities decode in prose: &amp; &mdash; &hellip;

> Markdown in, semantic HTML out. Nothing is ever assigned to innerHTML.

\`\`\`js
const tokens = Lexer.lex(md, { gfm: true });
\`\`\`

## What landed

- Tables with per-column alignment
- Task lists
  - Nested bullets keep their own list
- Strikethrough

1. Tokenize with marked
2. Walk the tokens
3. Build DOM nodes

- [x] GFM tables
- [x] Task lists
- [ ] Footnotes

| Feature | Status | Size |
| :------ | :----: | ---: |
| Tables  | Done   | 1 kB |
| Lists   | Done   | 2 kB |

---

![Puzzle Pieces badge](${BADGE})
`;

export default class MarkdownDoc extends PuzzleView {
	data(params, props) {
		return {
			sampleMd: SAMPLE_MD,
			installCmd,
			installNpm,
			usageImport,
			usageMarkup,
			usageNewTab,
			codeHero,
			toc: [
				{ label: 'Installation', href: '#installation' },
				{ label: 'Usage', href: '#usage' },
				{ label: 'Supported syntax', href: '#supported-syntax' },
				{ label: 'Security', href: '#security' },
			],
		};
	}
}
</script>
